From e5fd9c4b1709a54ab6cd1b521b949bdf0713befd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dpim <dpim_dpim@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:15:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] target/sparc: wake a halted strand on a pending interrupt,
 not a deliverable one

0023 gave the wr-form %asr26 write a real halt (cs->halted = 1) so that
plat_halt_strand() actually idles the strand instead of spinning. But
sparc_cpu_has_work() -- untouched upstream since 2013, and never reached
before 0023 because nothing else sets cs->halted on SPARC64 -- only asks
whether an interrupt is *deliverable right now*:

    return cpu_test_interrupt(cs, CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD) &&
           cpu_interrupts_enabled(cpu_env(cs));

A genuinely halted strand wakes on a *pending* interrupt, not a
deliverable one. PSTATE.IE (and, in hyperprivileged mode, HPSTATE.hpriv)
gate whether the trap may be *taken* once the strand resumes -- not
whether the hardware halt state may end. cpu_check_irqs() raises
CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD only once the trap can be taken, and the halt is
routinely entered with PSTATE.IE = 0 (an ordinary check-then-sleep idle
loop). So a %stick tick that fires and is correctly deferred by
cpu_check_irqs() (UA2005 table 12-3, "Conditioning of Disrupting Traps")
in the instant before the strand halts leaves SOFTINT.STIMER set with
nothing left to deliver it: has_work() returns false, the strand is
never re-evaluated, and it parks forever. The one-shot %stick_cmpr is
never re-armed because the guest's clock handler never runs, so there is
no periodic safety net behind it either -- one such tick is fatal.

Caught live and simultaneously on two independent guests: identical PC,
halted = 1, SOFTINT = 0x10000, %stick_cmpr timer disarmed
(expire_time == -1), zero host CPU, permanently.

Fix: when cs->halted, report work if any raw source is pending -- either
interrupt queue non-empty, pil_in set, softint set, or CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD
already raised. The non-halted path is unchanged. cpu_exec_halt() still
decides whether to actually leave the halt; this only has to say there is
a reason to run.

Needs 0004 (mondo-queues-asi-queue) for env->iqueue and the IQUEUE_*
offsets, and 0023 (which is the only thing that ever halts a SPARC64
strand). Confirmed: a >9 h idle that previously froze deterministically
on this exact signature now stays healthy on two stacks (cmd646/IDE and
mptsas/PCIe), SOFTINT clearing and the stick timer re-arming each tick.

AI-assisted.
---
 target/sparc/cpu.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/target/sparc/cpu.c b/target/sparc/cpu.c
index d2342ce98c..9fd7344788 100644
--- a/target/sparc/cpu.c
+++ b/target/sparc/cpu.c
@@ -785,8 +785,30 @@ static void sparc_restore_state_to_opc(CPUState *cs,
 #ifndef CONFIG_USER_ONLY
 static bool sparc_cpu_has_work(CPUState *cs)
 {
+    CPUSPARCState *env = cpu_env(cs);
+
+    /*
+     * A strand halted through STRAND_STS_REG.active (the wr-form %asr26
+     * write our hypervisor's plat_halt_strand() uses, 0023) is reactivated
+     * by a *pending* interrupt, not a deliverable one. cpu_check_irqs()
+     * raises CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD only once the trap may actually be taken,
+     * and the halt is routinely entered with PSTATE.IE = 0 (an ordinary
+     * check-then-sleep idle loop) or in hyperprivileged mode, where neither
+     * condition below can pass. Without examining the raw pending sources
+     * here, a tick that fired and was correctly deferred (UA2005 table
+     * 12-3) in the instant before the halt leaves the strand parked forever
+     * with SOFTINT still set. cpu_exec_halt() decides whether to actually
+     * leave the halt; this only reports whether there is a reason to.
+     */
+    if (cs->halted) {
+        return env->iqueue[IQUEUE_CPU_HEAD] != env->iqueue[IQUEUE_CPU_TAIL] ||
+               env->iqueue[IQUEUE_DEV_HEAD] != env->iqueue[IQUEUE_DEV_TAIL] ||
+               env->pil_in != 0 || env->softint != 0 ||
+               cpu_test_interrupt(cs, CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD);
+    }
+
     return cpu_test_interrupt(cs, CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD) &&
-           cpu_interrupts_enabled(cpu_env(cs));
+           cpu_interrupts_enabled(env);
 }
 #endif /* !CONFIG_USER_ONLY */
 
